# 02. Repository Context

## Purpose

Before implementing any feature, modifying any file, or making any engineering decision, develop a complete understanding of the repository.

Never begin implementation immediately after receiving a request.

Instead, understand the current state of the project first.

The repository is the single source of truth for implementation.

---

# Repository Overview

The repository is organized into independent but connected areas.

Each directory has a specific responsibility.

Never mix responsibilities between directories.

---

## Root Directory

The project root contains the overall project structure.

Example:

```text
codesense-ai/

backend/
frontend/
docs/
tasks/

README.md
CLAUDE.md
```

The root directory defines the entire software project.

No implementation files should be created here unless they belong at the project root.

---

# Backend

Location

```
backend/
```

Purpose

Contains the complete server-side application.

Responsibilities include:

- Business logic
- REST API
- Authentication
- AI orchestration
- Database
- GitHub integration
- File analysis
- Background jobs
- Services
- Models

All backend code must remain inside this directory.

Frontend code must never appear here.

---

# Frontend

Location

```
frontend/
```

Purpose

Contains the complete client-side application.

Responsibilities include:

- User Interface
- Dashboards
- Authentication Pages
- Project Explorer
- AI Chat
- Code Review UI
- Settings
- API Communication

Backend implementation must never exist inside the frontend.

---

# Documentation

Location

```
docs/
```

Purpose

Contains the permanent knowledge of the project.

Examples include:

- Project Vision
- Product Requirements
- System Architecture
- Technology Stack
- Folder Structure
- Database Design
- Coding Standards
- Development Rules

Documentation defines the intended system.

Implementation should follow the documentation whenever possible.

---

# Tasks

Location

```
tasks/
```

Purpose

Contains implementation specifications.

Tasks describe:

- objectives
- implementation steps
- acceptance criteria
- verification
- completion conditions

Tasks explain WHAT should be implemented.

They do not redefine project architecture.

---

# CLAUDE.md

Purpose

This file defines your operating procedures.

It explains:

- how to think
- how to work
- how to navigate the project
- how to make engineering decisions

This document defines your behaviour.

It does not define product requirements.

---

# README.md

Purpose

The README introduces the repository to developers.

It is intended for humans joining the project.

It should remain synchronized with the current state of development.

---

# Repository Responsibilities

Every directory has a single responsibility.

Never place files into unrelated directories.

Maintain clear separation of concerns.

When uncertain about file placement, follow the documented folder structure.

---

# Source of Truth

When multiple files discuss the same topic, follow this priority.

Highest Priority

1. CLAUDE.md

2. docs/

3. tasks/

4. Existing implementation

5. User request

If conflicts exist:

- Prefer documentation over assumptions.
- Prefer architecture over convenience.
- Request clarification when documentation conflicts.

---

# Repository Navigation Strategy

Before implementing any feature:

1. Read CLAUDE.md.

2. Read relevant documentation.

3. Read the roadmap.

4. Locate the implementation task.

5. Inspect existing code.

6. Understand dependencies.

7. Begin implementation.

Never skip repository analysis.

---

# Repository Integrity

Preserve consistency throughout the project.

Avoid:

- duplicate folders
- duplicate utilities
- duplicate implementations
- duplicate documentation

When improvements are possible:

- reuse existing code
- extend existing modules
- avoid unnecessary creation of new abstractions

---

# Architectural Respect

The repository structure represents architectural decisions.

Do not:

- rename folders
- reorganize modules
- relocate implementations

unless explicitly instructed.

Architecture changes require user approval.

---

# Repository Awareness

Always maintain awareness of:

- current implementation phase
- completed tasks
- active modules
- pending work
- project roadmap

Never work in isolation from the overall project.

Every implementation should fit naturally into the existing repository.

---

# Final Principle

Before writing code, understand the repository.

Before modifying code, understand the module.

Before creating new code, determine whether suitable functionality already exists.

The first responsibility of a professional engineer is understanding the system before changing it.
